  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nA shortwave rounding out the ridge axis today will develop scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms across mainly Southwest and Central MT \nthis afternoon and evening. Showers and thunderstorms are possible \nacross North-Central MT this evening, but coverage and intensity \nlook to be slightly less. Thunderstorms today will be on the drier \nside, with little precipitation reaching the ground. Strong \nmixing and a dry surface layer has resulted in DCAPE values \napproaching 1,000 j/kg. Strong gusty winds will be the primary \nhazard today. Thunderstorms have low confidence in becoming \nsevere, but I can't rule out an isolated severe wind gust due to \nthe strong mixing. \n\nIncreasing southwest flow due to the trough propagating east will \nadvect in better shear and moisture Thursday for better coverage of \nshowers and thunderstorms across the region. Steep lapse rates, \nstronger winds aloft mixing down, and modest shear will bring an \nisolated risk for severe wind gusts with thunderstorms. The \nincreasing winds aloft will keep breezy synoptic winds across the \nregion through the weekend until the trough passes through the \nregion. Friday, the thunderstorm threat shifts southeast to the \nDillon to Lewistown line. The breezy winds also bring elevated \nfire weather concerns Thursday and Friday.\n\nThe trough forms a closed low during the weekend which leads to \nbetter chances for scattered to widespread precipitation. There \nremains some uncertainty in the track of this low, which \nultimately effects thunderstorm chances Saturday and Sunday. \nRecent runs have trended down on the chances of higher peak \nmountain snow Sunday morning, but I can't completely rule it out \nif the low track and cold air advection changes. Below normal \ntemperatures will also limit severity of thunderstorms. Behind \nthis wave, another trough forms along the West Coast U.S. However,\ndifference in timing and amplitude leave details uncertain, but \ncurrent forecast trends to cooler and some precipitation \ncontinuing.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nModels continue to show 30-50kts shear, steep lapse rates, and \nstrong vertical mixing tomorrow to increase confidence in isolated \nsevere thunderstorms to form. Severe wind gusts will be the main \nhazard, but with steep lapse rates and elevated CAPE focused in the \nhail growth zone, I can't rule out isolated large hail. PWATs \nbetween 0.70\"-0.90\" will bring a mix of wet and dry thunderstorms,\nwith wet thunderstorms developing with the stronger storms. \n\nCooler temperatures this weekend tamper thunderstorm chances \nSaturday despite strong southwest flow from the incoming trough. The \ncurrent closed low track has northwest cold air advection Sunday,\nwhich is not favorable for thunderstorms. However, a few runs \nshift the track a little south, keeping us in southwest flow with \nbetter chances for storms. -Wilson",
